===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|redel}}, {{m|enm|redels}}, from {{inh|en|ang|rǣdels}}, {{m|ang|rǣdelse|t=counsel, opinion, imagination, riddle}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*rādislī|t=counsel, conjecture}}. Analyzable as {{af|en|rede|t1=advice|-le}}. Akin to {{cog|ang|rǣdan||to read, advise, interpret}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|raadsel}}, {{cog|de|Rätsel}}.

====Noun====
{{examples|: '''6th c. {{BCE}}''', Laozi, ''{{w|Tao Te Ching}}'' 9:
:: Keep sharpening the blade, you'll soon blunt it.
: {{RQ:King James Version|Luke|17|33}}
:: Whosoever shall seek to save his life shall lose it; and whosoever shall lose his life shall preserve it.
|sense=ancient form}}
{{en-noun}}

# A [[verbal]] [[puzzle]], [[mystery]], or other [[problem]] of an [[intellectual]] nature.
#: {{syn|en|enigma|conundrum|brain-teaser}}
#: {{ux|en|Here's a '''riddle''': It's black, and white, and red all over. What is it?}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Judges|14|12|14|passage=12 ¶ And Samson said vnto them, I will now put foorth a '''riddle''' vnto you: if you can certeinly declare it me, within the seuen dayes of the feast, and finde it out, then I will giue you thirtie sheetes, and thirtie change of garments:<br>13 But if ye cannot declare it me, then shall yee giue me thirtie sheetes, and thirtie change of garments. And they said vnto him, Put foorth thy '''riddle''', that we may heare it.<br>14 And hee said vnto them, Out of the eater came foorth meate, aud out of the strong came foorth sweetnesse. And they could not in three dayes expound the '''riddle'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Regained|book=Samson Agonistes|page=72|passage=To wring from me and tell to them my ſecret, / That ſolv'd the '''riddle''' which I had propos'd.}}
#* {{RQ:Chambers Younger Set|VIII|passage=Elbows almost touching they leaned at ease, idly reading the almost obliterated lines engraved there. &para; {{nowrap|"I never}} understood it," she observed, lightly scornful. "What occult meaning has a sun-dial for the spooney? ''I'm'' sure I don't want to read '''riddles''' in a strange gentleman's optics."}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1992|artist={{w|Alice in Chains|Alice in Chains}}|title={{w|Dirt (Alice in Chains album)|Rain When I Die}}|passage=I'm a '''riddle''' so strong, you can't break me}}
# An ancient verbal, poetic, or literary form, in which, rather than a [[rhyme]] scheme, there are parallel opposing expressions with a hidden meaning.

