===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|riddil}}, {{m|enm|ridelle||sieve}}, from {{inh|en|ang|hriddel||sieve}}, alteration of earlier {{m|ang|hridder}}, {{m|ang|hrīder}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*hrīdrā}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*hrīdrą}}, {{m|gem-pro|*hrīdrǭ||sieve}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*hrid-||to shake}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*krey-}}. Akin to {{cog|de|Reiter||sieve}}, {{cog|non|hreinn||pure, clean}}, {{cog|goh|hreini||pure, clean}}, {{cog|got|𐌷𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃||clean, pure}}. More at {{l|en|rinse}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|sieve}} A [[sieve]] with coarse [[mesh]]es, usually of wire, for separating coarser materials from finer, as chaff from grain, cinders from ashes, or gravel from sand.
# A [[board]] with a row of [[pin]]s, set [[zigzag]], between which wire is drawn to [[straighten]] it.

=====Translations=====
{{trans-see|sieve}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To put something through a [[#Noun 2|riddle]] or [[sieve#Noun|sieve]]; to [[sieve#Verb|sieve]]; to [[sift#Verb|sift]].
#: {{ux|en|You have to '''riddle''' the gravel before you lay it on the road.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Helen Yemm|title=Thorny problems: How can I revive a forsythia hedge? &#91;print version 5 April 2014, p.&nbsp;G9&#93;|url=http://www.telegraph.co.uk/gardening/gardeningadvice/10743118/Thorny-problems-how-can-I-revive-a-forsythia-hedge.html|newspaper={{w|The Daily Telegraph}} (Gardening)|location=London|date=8 April 2014|passage=In its finest form&nbsp;– two years old or more&nbsp;– leaf mould can be '''riddled''' (sieved) and used, mixed 50/50 with sand, to make fine potting compost for seeds and cuttings.}}
# To [[fill]] with [[hole]]s like a [[#Noun 2|riddle]].
#: {{ux|en|The shots from his gun began to '''riddle''' the targets.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1980|title=AA Book of British Villages|publisher=Drive Publications Ltd|page=144|text=New Alresford is constantly mispronounced, T-shaped, honeycombed with cellars, packed with antique shops, '''riddled''' with woodworm, surrounded by watercress. The name - which means 'ford by the alders' - is pronounced 'Allsford'; and no one ever uses 'New', though they do call the adjoining village Old Alresford.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|author=Drachinifel|title=The Battle of Samar - Odds? What are those?|date=27 February 2019|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4AdcvDiA3lE|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20221103043318/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4AdcvDiA3lE|archivedate=3 November 2022|accessdate=5 November 2022|time=29:08|text=''Kalinin Bay'' is also in trouble, trading fire with Japanese destroyers and taking hits from both them and cruisers at the same time.  Unlike the ''Gambier Bay'', however, it does not appear that ''these'' ships have realized they need to switch to high explosive from armor-piercing, and, despite being '''riddled''' with shellfire, the ship stays afloat, despite this rather-unequal battering going on for another twenty to thirty minutes.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} To [[fill]] or [[spread]] [[throughout]]; to [[pervade]] (with something destructive or weakening).
#: {{ux|en|Your argument is '''riddled''' with errors.}}
#* {{RQ:Donne Devotions|chapter=1. Meditation|page=8|passage=O perplex'd diſcompoſition, O '''ridling''' diſtemper, O miſerable condition of Man.|footer=An adjective use.}}

